implementationPlan.description:
WITHHOLD WHEN NO TARGET IS BOUND (DoD 2), and WRITE THE GRANULARITY DECISION DOWN (DoD 3).

WITHHOLDING: emit the deployment offer only when a target IS bound for the goal's project. Read it through the landed reader rather than re-deriving - the durable target is `deploy-target:<projectId>:<environment>` (deploy-target-contracts.ts:84) and `DeployTargetPort = (environment) => DeployTarget | null` (deploy-ports.ts:41). With the environment chosen at dispatch, "bound" means at least one environment has a target.
WITHHELD MEANS ABSENT, NOT DISABLED (rail 2): no offer at all, so the consumer's card is absent rather than a dead button in front of an operator.

THE COMMENT DoD 3 REQUIRES, at the offer site, stating the decision and its evidence:
- the offer is ONE PER GOAL at `deploy:<goalId>`;
- the ENVIRONMENT IS CHOSEN AT DISPATCH and travels in the payload - `daemon-command-payload-keys.ts:166` declares `deployment.deploy` payload keys as `["environment", "sha"]`, so a per-environment offer would put the environment in two places that can disagree;
- the durable target is keyed per (project, environment), a DIFFERENT axis that deliberately does not appear in the offer identity;
- the card reads this and renders its per-environment rows itself.
Write it as reasoning, not as a restatement of what the code does - the next reader needs to know why the two granularities differ, which is the question that made this row necessary.

THE MUTATION DRILL (DoD 6) - it reproduces exactly the defect this row exists to fix, so it is the row's most valuable artifact.

Target the offer at the PROJECT aggregate instead of `deploy:<goalId>` - i.e. restore the fallthrough behaviour - and confirm the DoD-1 arm goes RED. QUOTE THE FAILING LINE. That failing line is the defect as it exists at HEAD today: the surface believes it offered something and the card renders nothing.

RESTORE PER GLOBAL RAIL 13, which is stricter than the usual `git checkout --`:
1. `git status --porcelain` the exact path FIRST;
2. back the file up OUT OF TREE (%TEMP%, suffixed by your worker id);
3. restore FROM THE BACKUP, not with `git checkout --`;
4. prove the restore by SHA256 equality in a SEPARATE call - a PowerShell copy can silently no-op, and "the command returned" is not evidence the bytes moved.

Also drill the WITHHOLDING arm: bind no target, force the offer to be emitted anyway, and confirm the DoD-2 set assertion reds. A withholding rule that has never been seen red is not evidence, and this is the one that keeps a dead button away from an operator.
